## ex2 - detecting `any` and `unknown`

`any` is the type that lies. It is assignable to everything and everything is
assignable to it, which makes every ordinary `extends` check useless on it. The
standard detector exploits exactly that.

Edit `ex2/solution.ts`. Implement:

```ts
export type IsAny<T> = /* ... */;
export type IsUnknown<T> = /* ... */;
```

Expected:

```ts
type _1 = Expect<Equal<IsAny<any>, true>>;
type _2 = Expect<Equal<IsAny<unknown>, false>>;
type _3 = Expect<Equal<IsAny<never>, false>>;
type _4 = Expect<Equal<IsUnknown<unknown>, true>>;
type _5 = Expect<Equal<IsUnknown<any>, false>>;
type _6 = Expect<Equal<IsUnknown<never>, false>>;
type _7 = Expect<Equal<IsUnknown<{}>, false>>;
```

`IsUnknown<any>` is the assertion that costs people the exercise: `unknown`
extends `any` and `any` extends `unknown`, so a bidirectional check calls `any`
unknown unless you rule it out first.

```
